From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:37:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/h264: finish the dependent view before an IDR empties
 the views

A packet can begin with the dependent view of the access unit before it
(the layout of some Matroska files, which ff_h264_resolve_view_pair()
deals with). The picture of that view is still open when the first
slice of the base view is met, and the switch to the base view in
ff_h264_queue_decode_slice() finishes it: ff_h264_field_end() marks it
as a reference of its view and takes its POC and frame_num as the ones
that the view goes on from.

Where that slice is an IDR slice, decode_nal_units() calls idr() as it
meets the NAL unit, before the slice is queued, and so before the
switch. idr() empties every view; the switch then put the last picture
of the dependent view of the coded video sequence that ends there back
among the references of that view, and the POC of the view went on from
it. The first picture of the dependent view in the new sequence had that
picture in its reference list (an anchor picture whose P slices use one
reference: the stale temporal one in place of the inter-view one) and
was decoded from it, and so were the pictures predicted from it, until
the view refreshed. With one access unit a packet the dependent view is
finished at the end of the packet before, and nothing of this happens.
The comment of idr() on frame_num saw a part of it. The series of
August does the same.

Now the picture of the dependent view is finished, and the switch to the
base view made, before idr() runs, as the slice would make them a
moment later. If that fails, idr() still runs and the slice is dropped,
as before. Nothing else changes: the function does nothing in the base
view, which is all that a stream of one view has.

Measured with the series as of 2026-10-14, carried to FFmpeg 9.0.2,
fixes 9002 to 9008, on the host, the decoder fed packet by packet as a
player feeds it, against the reference decoder (JM 19.1): the 960x540
stream of two views of the reference encoder twice, the stream of fix
9006 twice (scaling matrices in the subset SPS), and that stream with
its subset SPS replaced and the PPS not sent again. In the layout with
the dependent view in the next packet, without this change 11 of the 50
pictures of the second view differ from JM, from the IDR on, with one
thread, 8 frame threads and 8 slice threads; with it, both layouts give
JM's pictures. Unchanged: every view of the 27 ITU-T H.264.1 MVC streams
and of the streams made for fixes 9006 to 9008 against JM, the late
second view of fix 9005, the flushes of fix 9002 (480 runs, no crash,
the same pictures), the base views decoded as streams of one view (the
same as the stock decoder), and no report from AddressSanitizer or
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
---
 libavcodec/h264dec.c | 44 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libavcodec/h264dec.c b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
index 935b291f0bdb..6e7e73efc932 100644
--- a/libavcodec/h264dec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
@@ -696,6 +696,47 @@ static void debug_green_metadata(const H264SEIGreenMetaData *gm, void *logctx)
     }
 }
 
+/**
+ * Finish the picture of the dependent view that a packet began with, as the
+ * switch to the base view in ff_h264_queue_decode_slice() does, before idr()
+ * empties the views for an IDR access unit.
+ *
+ * A packet can begin with the dependent view of the access unit before it (the
+ * layout that ff_h264_resolve_view_pair() deals with). The picture of that view
+ * is still open when the first slice of the base view is met, and the switch to
+ * the base view finishes it: ff_h264_field_end() marks it as a reference of its
+ * view and takes its POC and frame_num as the ones the view goes on from. Where
+ * that slice is an IDR slice, idr() runs before it is queued, and so before the
+ * switch: the picture of the coded video sequence that ends there was then put
+ * back among the references of the view that idr() had just emptied, and the
+ * POC of that view went on from it. The first picture of the dependent view in
+ * the new sequence was predicted from it.
+ */
+static int finish_dependent_view(H264Context *h)
+{
+    int ret;
+
+    if (!h->cur_view || !h->cur_pic_ptr || !h->current_slice)
+        return 0;
+
+    if (h->nb_slice_ctx_queued) {
+        ret = ff_h264_execute_decode_slices(h);
+        if (ret < 0 && (h->avctx->err_recognition & AV_EF_EXPLODE))
+            return ret;
+    }
+
+    ret = ff_h264_field_end(h, h->slice_ctx, 1);
+    if (ret < 0)
+        return ret;
+    if (!h->droppable || h->views_active_decode != 1)
+        ff_thread_report_progress(&h->cur_pic_ptr->tf, INT_MAX,
+                                  h->picture_structure == PICT_BOTTOM_FIELD);
+    h->current_slice = 0;
+    if (!(FIELD_PICTURE(h) && h->first_field))
+        h->first_field = 0;
+    return ff_h264_view_switch_fields(h, 0);
+}
+
 static int decode_nal_units(H264Context *h, AVBufferRef *buf_ref,
                             const uint8_t *buf, int buf_size)
 {
@@ -778,10 +819,13 @@ static int decode_nal_units(H264Context *h, AVBufferRef *buf_ref,
                 goto end;
             }
             if(!idr_cleared) {
+                err = finish_dependent_view(h);
                 idr(h); // FIXME ensure we don't lose some frames if there is reordering
             }
             idr_cleared = 1;
             h->has_recovery_point = 1;
+            if (err < 0)
+                break;
             av_fallthrough;
         case H264_NAL_SLICE:
         case H264_NAL_EXTEN_SLICE:
